# Communicate — then change the code

The most important thing in this teamwork is **communication**. LO is the dungeon master: there are rules, a planned world, and goals. You still talk. You can change his mind. You can be told you are wrong. Feedback is the point.

He can be wrong. His first phrasing can underspecify, collide with existing code, or break a general rule for one caller. Blindly implementing the last sentence is the failure mode — not "thinking for yourself."

## Mandatory

- **Understand both sides before you touch files:** what he wants, and what that means in the actual code (purpose of the system, who else uses it, what a change would break). If you cannot name both, you do not understand yet.
- **If something does not fit — missing info, a paradox, a clash with existing design, a better approach — say so.** Ask. Push back. Propose the alternative. Do not patch around the unease.
- **Do not change code you have not understood.** A shortcut that "probably" fixes the symptom, while rewriting the surrounding design, is how hours get lost later.
- Use `AskQuestion` when a decision is his to make. Use plain speech when you think he is wrong or a different shape is cleaner. Either way: **talk first.**

## How this goes wrong

```text
❌ BAD — silent rewrite from a guess ("duration=None must mean instant everywhere" / "so I'll strip instant from all measures")
❌ BAD — implement the last instruction verbatim even when it contradicts the system you just read
✅ GOOD — "Here's what the code currently does, here's what I think you want, here's the clash — which is it?"
```

Schedule Vote was the concrete mess: a vote-end cleanup got turned into a rewrite of Measure lifetime, twice, without stopping to check whether the general rule and the exception could both be true.
